# Cmd/Ctrl+K search shortcut design

## Recommendation

Keep the shortcut in `SearchPage.svelte`'s existing `shortcut` handler. The page already owns the search input reference, `/` behavior, Escape behavior, and a `<svelte:window onkeydown={shortcut}>` that exists only while the search route is mounted. Adding a reusable Svelte action would move a one-page policy behind an abstraction and still require the page to supply the input and platform policy. A tiny named platform choice in the component is enough.

Use one value as the source for both matching and hint rendering:

```ts
type SearchShortcut = {
  modifier: "metaKey" | "ctrlKey";
  hint: "⌘ K" | "Ctrl K";
};

const searchShortcut: SearchShortcut =
  /Mac|iPhone|iPad|iPod/.test(navigator.platform)
    ? { modifier: "metaKey", hint: "⌘ K" }
    : { modifier: "ctrlKey", hint: "Ctrl K" };
```

The platform decision is named and atomic: the same selected modifier used in the event check selects the visible hint. Put the hint in a sibling `<kbd>` beside the existing `/` key, or replace `/` with the platform hint if the design only wants one shortcut advertised. Keeping both in the UI is clearest because both shortcuts remain supported. Make the new hint visible when the query is nonempty too; the current conditional hides `/` once text is entered, which would otherwise make the new advertised shortcut disappear at the most useful time. An accessible text label may say “Focus search (⌘K)” / “Focus search (Ctrl K)”; current `<kbd>` is aria-hidden, so the hint is decorative only.

## Event rules

Add the new focus case before the existing `/` and Escape cases. Require `event.key.toLowerCase() === "k"`, the selected modifier, and no other modifier (`!event.shiftKey`, `!event.altKey`, and `!event.getModifierState("AltGraph")` if browser support is part of the target). Reject the shortcut while `event.isComposing` or `composing` is true. Do not reject merely because an input, textarea, select, or contenteditable owns focus: Cmd/Ctrl+K is an explicit focus-search command, so it should work while editing and preserve the current query. Call `preventDefault()` and `input.focus()`, but do not clear or select the input value.

For strict modifier matching, on Mac require `metaKey && !ctrlKey`; elsewhere require `ctrlKey && !metaKey`. This prevents Ctrl+Cmd+K from being treated as either platform shortcut. Alt and Shift must be absent on both platforms. `event.key` is case-insensitive to account for keyboard layout and capitalization. Avoid requiring `event.code === "KeyK"`, which is less layout-friendly.

Leave `/` and Escape logic unchanged. In particular, `/` should still avoid all editable targets and all Ctrl/Meta/Alt combinations, and Escape should still clear only when the search input is the target and composition is over. Focusing with Cmd/Ctrl+K never changes the query or URL.

